Q: Getting updated value of Textbox in asp.net and c# A: <p>According to the ASP.NET's life cycle, <code>Page_Load</code> executes before <code>Button2_Click</code>, so you have to  show your updated text in the <code>Button2_Click</code>:</p>
<pre><code>protected void Page_Load(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    contentID = Convert.ToInt32(Request.QueryString[&quot;contentid&quot;]);
    if (contentID != 0)
    {
        if (!this.IsPostBack)
        {
            getContentBody(contentID);
            TextBox1.Text = content;
            msg_lbl.Text = &quot;Inside not PostBack&quot;;
        }
    }
    else
    {
        Response.Write(&quot;Invalid URL for article&quot;);
    }
}

public void getContentBody(int contentID)
{
    try
    {
        //////////////Opening the connection///////////////

        mycon.Open();
        string str = &quot;select content from content where contentID='&quot; + contentID + &quot;'&quot;;
        //Response.Write(str);
        MySqlCommand command1 = mycon.CreateCommand();
        command1.CommandText = str;
        dr = command1.ExecuteReader();
        if (dr.Read())
        {
            content = dr[0].ToString();
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Response.Write(&quot;Exception reading data&quot; + ex);
    }
    finally
    {
        dr.Close();
        mycon.Close();
    }
}

protected void Button2_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    //string textboxvalue = Request.Form[TextBox1.UniqueID];

    mycon.Open();
    string query = &quot;update content set content='&quot; + TextBox1.Text + &quot;' where contentID= '&quot; + contentID + &quot;'&quot;;
    msg_lbl.Text = query;
    try
    {
        MySqlCommand command1 = mycon.CreateCommand();
        command1.CommandText = query;
        command1.ExecuteNonQuery();
        getContentBody(contentID);
        TextBox1.Text = content;

        msg_lbl.Text = &quot;text&quot; + TextBox1.Text;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        msg_lbl.Text = &quot;Exception in saving data&quot; + ex;
    }
    finally
    {
        mycon.Close();    
    } 
}
</code></pre>

Q: Button on modal does not work when modal is closed and re-opened A: <p><strong>The Problem</strong></p>
<p>​The issue occurs because when you close the modal, you manually set modalSettingsMenu.style.display = none However, the click event listener logic for the settings button might be failing to correctly evaluate the property the next time the modal is opened, especially if the initial CSS state wasn't explicitly defined in the inline styles.</p>
<p><strong>The Solution</strong></p>
<p>To ensure the button works every time, you should check for both none and an empty string which is the default value before any JS interaction.</p>
<p><strong>Updated JavaScript</strong></p>
<p>Update your event listener to this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>modalSettingsButton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function() {
    // Check if it's hidden or not set yet
    if (modalSettingsMenu.style.display === &quot;none&quot; || modalSettingsMenu.style.display === &quot;&quot;) {
        modalSettingsMenu.style.display = &quot;block&quot;;
    } else {
        modalSettingsMenu.style.display = &quot;none&quot;;
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why this happens?</strong></p>
<p>When you reset the display to none in your window.onclick function, the toggle logic needs to be robust enough to recognize that change. By checking for  empty string, you handle the initial state of the element before the first click.</p>
<p><strong>Pro Tip Using ClassList Cleaner Approach</strong></p>
<p>A more modern and less error-prone way is to use a CSS class:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.hidden { display: none; }
.visible { display: block; }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>JavaScript:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>modalSettingsButton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function() {
    modalSettingsMenu.classList.toggle(&quot;visible&quot;);
});
</code></pre>
